## Our Takeaway

Based on reported disclosures from [SpaceX](https://ir.spacex.com/updates/releases-details/2026/SpaceX-to-Invest-in-the-Next-Generation-of-Americans-2026-r_LYQ5cH94/default.aspx), reporting from [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/25/spacex-musk-trump-account-stock.html), and guidance on [trumpaccounts.gov](https://trumpaccounts.gov), you should claim your child's Trump Account to secure the $1,000 seed deposit and unlock family or employer contributions, not because any source says claiming earns the stock. Neither the SpaceX announcement nor CNBC defines what makes an account "active", and no published source confirms a transfer date, valuation, or claim requirement for the corporate gift. In addition, CNBC reports, citing Treasury rules, that stock gifts cannot be refused and generally cannot be sold until the earlier of five years or the end of the calendar year in which the beneficiary turns 17.

If you want guaranteed stock ownership today, this process is not for you, and you should open a [custodial brokerage account](/guides/how-to-open-a-custodial-brokerage-account-for-kids/) instead of waiting on unconfirmed corporate gifts. Our takeaway would change if SpaceX or the Treasury Department defines "active", states a formal claim requirement, schedules an official distribution date, or publishes regulatory text under Treasury Decision 10056 with different sale rules. Until official terms appear, complete your identity verification on [trumpaccounts.gov](https://trumpaccounts.gov) to claim the available cash seed rather than banking on the unconfirmed SpaceX share for kids.